      "Specificity wars come from styles with no agreed order, so each team out-ranks the last with a longer selector or !important. The modern fix is to make the order explicit instead of competing on weight.",
      "Cascade layers do that. Declare the order once, @layer reset, design-system, components, utilities, app, and a rule in a later layer wins over an earlier one regardless of selector specificity. Unlayered styles beat every layer, which is useful for migrating: put the legacy CSS into a low layer and new code wins by default. One trap: !important inverts the layer order, so an important declaration in an early layer beats one in a later layer.",
      "Inside a library, :where() wraps selectors at zero specificity, so a design system's defaults are trivially overridable by any consumer class. :is() is the opposite, it takes the highest specificity of its arguments, which surprises people.",
      "@scope limits styles to a subtree, with an optional lower boundary, which covers much of what people used CSS Modules or BEM naming for; check it against your browser support matrix before relying on it.",
