      "Deploy in that order: upload the new assets first, then switch index.html. And keep old assets around for days, not minutes. Users with an open tab are still running the old index and will lazy-load old chunks when they navigate; delete those and they get a chunk-load error. Catch that error anyway and offer a reload.",
      "Rollback is then cheap: point index.html back at the previous build's assets, which are still there. No rebuild, seconds not minutes. If rollback means rebuilding from an older commit, it is too slow for an incident.",
      "Version skew is the hidden risk. Old clients keep running for hours after a deploy, so the API has to stay compatible with at least the previous frontend: expand-then-contract changes, never remove a field the old client reads in the same release.",
      "And decouple deploy from release with feature flags, so turning off a broken feature does not need a deploy at all. A service worker needs its own update strategy, or it will keep serving the old version.",
